course: dictionary
lesson: 2974
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The word dry is a good example: it can mean not wet, or it can mean boring. Context tells you which meaning the writer wants. Read the words around it and ask: what does dry mean in this sentence?
explain: «Hang the towels outside so they dry in the sun.» Here dry means to make or become dry, so the towels lose their water. The clues are hang and sun. Try the meaning in the sentence: the towels become not wet. It makes sense.
explain: «The book was so dry that I fell asleep.» Dry does not mean without water here. The clue is fell asleep: the book was boring. Dry can also describe humour. «That's not the worst idea you've had» is a dry way of saying «good idea», because it sounds serious but means something kind.
explain: Pick the meaning that fits the sentence best, then check it by putting it back in. If the meaning makes the sentence silly, try another one.
fact: Dry can describe something, as in «dry clothes», or it can mean to make something dry, as in «dry your hands».
fact: Dry can mean not wet, with little or no rain, thirsty, boring, or funny in a quiet, serious way.
fact: «A dry summer» means a summer with little or no rain.
fact: «Dry humour» is funny in a quiet, serious way, so the joke can be hard to spot.
fact: Some phrases, such as «dry up» and «dry off», use dry in a way that is different from its single-word meanings.
exercise: «Please dry your hands after washing.» What does dry mean here? | To be boring | To be thirsty | *To make something dry | To make something no longer wet
exercise: «My mouth is dry after the long run.» Which meaning of dry fits? | Not wet | Boring | Funny in a quiet way | *Thirsty
exercise: «It was a dry summer, with no rain for weeks.» What does dry mean? | Very funny | Covered in water | *With little or no rain | Very loud
test: «The lecture was so dry that half the class dozed off.» What does dry mean? | Very hot | Full of rain | Covered in dust | *Boring
test: Dad said, «That's not the worst idea you've had.» What kind of humour does dry describe in «dry humour»? | Very loud and silly | *Funny in a quiet, serious way | Ugly and rude | Wet and soggy
exam: «The grass was dry and crunchy after weeks of sun.» What does dry mean here? | Very funny | Soft and wet | Full of water | *Without water, from the heat and sun
